From 9b496dbf1d7f5163efb7cda8c9dc97e3445e57ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 02:14:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/29] Make the guard able to see a crash without the boot
 reason

The first run on the phone proved three things that were only theory before,
and all three were the guard failing to see:

  boot reason: not readable [UNKNOWN]      -> the latch never engaged
  last probe: ... - passed: the submit stage could not be built: AttributeError
                                           -> no GPU test was ever run
  sample: frames -1ms, wedged 0 (x5)       -> the watchdog was inert

One. The boot reason cannot be read on this device: android.os.SystemProperties
is a hidden API and returns nothing, and all five fallback keys were properties
too. So the guard now keeps its own record - the session it started, with the
uptime and the wall clock at that moment - and proves a reboot from it: uptime
is monotonic and only resets across a boot, so an uptime now behind the uptime
the session began at (or behind where the wall clock says it should be, with ten
minutes for clock steps) is a restart, proved. It says more than the property
would have: not "the phone restarted" but "a session of ours was running when it
did". getprop is added as a second source, and PowerManager's shutdown reason as
a third; the report now names which source answered and says plainly when none
did.

Two. The probe asked the ICD, through vk_icdGetInstanceProcAddr, for device-level
entry points before any instance existed; an ICD may answer NULL for those, and
this one did. The NULL was then reported by a broken error path (a .decode() on a
str), which turned the whole run into a "skip", and a skip is allowed through -
so a session started with no GPU test at all, and the word for that in the report
was "passed". The probe now goes through the loader, which is what dispatches
entry points and what the session itself uses; the driver is still the one named
by VK_DRIVER_FILES. Skips keep their verdict apart from "ok" everywhere: the
report says "SKIPPED, NO GPU TEST WAS RUN", the page marks them "?" rather than
a tick, and the traceback goes to the log with stage markers saying how far the
probe got before it stopped.

Three. The wedged-process check only ran once a frame had been presented, so for
the first twenty-odd seconds of every session - the part where Steam warms up and
the part where this device has faulted every time - the watchdog watched nothing.
It is now armed eight seconds after the session starts, whatever the compositor
has done. The frame budget is capped at 2000 ms: the kernel gives a fence 3000 ms
before it dumps and panics, so any larger budget is a detector guaranteed to
arrive after the reboot it exists to prevent. The sweep behind an abort drops
from four seconds to 1.2 s for the same reason.

And the report now answers "is this protection actually working" rather than "is
it switched on": which source proved the last crash, whether the compositor has
ever reported a frame interval, and whether the GPU was tested at all.

